[{"record_id":"rec_000233","title":"BulkFormer: A large-scale foundation model for bulk transcriptomes","abstract":"Foundation models are transforming transcriptome analysis, yet most RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) foundation models are pretrained on sparse single-cell RNA-seq data, which typically captures only ∼3,000 genes per cell. This creates a need for models tailored to bulk transcriptomes, a modality that profiles ∼16,000 genes per sample and supports clinical and tissue-level analyses. Here, we present BulkFormer, a foundation model for bulk transcriptome analysis. BulkFormer contains ∼150 million parameters, covers 20,010 protein-coding genes, and is pretrained on 581,503 human bulk RNA-seq profiles. Its hybrid encoder combines a graph neural network to model explicit gene-gene relationships with a Performer module to capture global expression dependencies. Across five downstream tasks, BulkFormer outperformed existing single-cell foundation models while requiring substantially lower training cost. These results identify pretraining data modality as a key determinant of foundation model performance and establish BulkFormer as a framework for bulk transcriptome modeling. A record of this paper's transparent peer review process is included in the supplemental information.","doi":"10.1016/j.cels.2026.101657","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000234","title":"AI-Integrated Single-Cell Multi-Omics Decodes the Hepatocellular Carcinoma Metabolism-Immune Axis: A New Strategy for Precision Therapeutic Targeting","abstract":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors have improved outcomes for hepatocellular carcinoma, yet most patients do not respond because the tumor’s metabolic environment suppresses immune cells. Single-cell RNA sequencing has revealed extensive immune diversity, but conventional analyses cannot link cell states to their physical location or to the metabolic signals that drive dysfunction.\n \n \n \n In this review, we examine how artificial intelligence, combined with single-cell and spatial multi-omics, can decode the metabolism-immunity network in liver cancer. We highlight two key metabolic switches: lipid uptake through a scavenger receptor that triggers ferroptosis in killer T cells, and lactate-induced changes in gene regulation that lock macrophages into a tumor-promoting state. We also summarize advanced computational tools including deep learning for data integration, spatial deconvolution, and foundation models that can infer metabolic activity from single-cell data and reconstruct cell movement over time.\n \n \n \n These approaches enable researchers to identify key metabolic drivers of immune evasion and predict which checkpoints are most actionable.\n \n \n \n Artificial-intelligence-driven multi-omics transforms hepatocellular carcinoma research from descriptive catalogues into predictive, mechanism-based models, offering a roadmap for designing next-generation immunotherapies.","doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1915323","year":"","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000235","title":"The Model as Microscope: Knowledge Discovery via Mechanistic Interpretability of Scientific Foundation Models","abstract":"Foundation models trained on scientific data-protein and DNA sequences, single-cell transcriptomes, molecules, and physical systems-have become implicit repositories of domain knowledge, encoding regularities they were never explicitly taught. Mechanistic interpretability (MI), the study of the internal computations of trained networks, offers a route to read that knowledge out: not merely to explain a prediction, but to convert a model's learned representations and circuits into testable scientific hypotheses. This review surveys the use of mechanistic interpretability as an instrument of knowledge discovery on scientific models. We organize the MI toolbox along a spectrum from correlational to causal: probing and concept-activation methods; feature attribution and visualization; sparse dictionary learning with sparse autoencoders; and the causal core of modern MI-activation and attribution patching, causal mediation and abstraction, automated circuit discovery, and causal scrubbing. We then trace how these methods have yielded genuine domain insight: protein language models whose attention recovers folding contacts and whose representations forecast viral immune escape; single-cell foundation models whose sparse features encode cell-type programs and whose causal circuits reveal inhibitory-dominated, biologically coherent, cross-model-convergent computation; genomic models whose features map to regulatory grammar; and, as proofs of concept, sequence models shown to learn explicit world models and interpretable algorithms, and deep networks distilled into symbolic physical laws. We give particular weight to biological foundation models, where mature annotation databases supply the external ground truth that interpretability elsewhere lacks. Throughout we stress the epistemic hazard at the center of the enterprise-the gap between a plausible interpretation and a true mechanism-and argue that knowledge discovery via MI is credible only when interpretations are stated precisely, validated against external knowledge, and, wherever possible, confirmed by intervention.","doi":"10.2139/ssrn.6967579","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000236","title":"Medea: An AI agent for therapeutic reasoning across biological contexts","abstract":"Therapeutic hypotheses can transfer across diseases but their relevance depends on biological context. The same target, perturbation, or treatment can produce different effects across cell types, disease states, genetic backgrounds, and patients. Therapeutic reasoning therefore requires methods that preserve context, test when evidence supports transfer, and identify where context-specific effects limit it. Although AI agents can perform therapeutic analyses, existing systems often fail to preserve biological context over long workflows, verify intermediate computational steps, or reconcile conflicting evidence across datasets and literature. Here, we present Medea, an AI agent for therapeutic reasoning across biological contexts. Medea executes multi-step analyses using biological tools, machine learning models, and literature retrieval while enforcing verification during planning, execution, and evidence synthesis. We evaluate Medea across 5,673 open-ended analyses in three domains: cell type specific therapeutic target nomination in five diseases and 29 cell types, synthetic lethality prediction in 7 cancer cell lines, and immunotherapy response prediction from multimodal patient profiles. Using a previously unpublished epistatic miniarray profiling screen performed under two DNA-damaging treatments, we evaluate Medea on predicting synthetic lethality among 238,046 gene-gene pairs in yeast. Medea predicts these experimentally measured synthetic lethal interactions, indicating that its performance reflects biological relevance rather than information leakage from benchmark datasets. Across these evaluations, Medea improves performance over large language models, reasoning models, biomedical agents, and specialized machine learning models while maintaining low failure rates and calibrated abstention. These results show that verifiable AI agents can perform therapeutic analyses across biological contexts.","doi":"10.64898/2026.01.16.696667","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000237","title":"Large Language Models in Drug Discovery and Development: Multimodal Applications, Translational Challenges, and Future Perspectives","abstract":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used as knowledge-integration, hypothesisgeneration, molecular-representation, clinical-workflow, and documentation-support systems across drug discovery and development. This review adopts a full-pipeline perspective, spanning target identification, molecular generation, virtual screening, ADMET and toxicity assessment, clinical-trial optimization, pharmacology support, pharmacovigilance, regulatory documentation, and emerging agentic workflows. To avoid a simple accumulation of application scenarios, the article organizes the literature around a central analytical thread: how different LLM paradigms create different levels of pharmaceutical value, evidence maturity, validation burden, and translational risk. We distinguish biomedical text LLMs, molecular language models, protein and omics language models, multimodal molecular systems, retrieval-augmented generation systems, and tool-augmented or agentic workflows. For each paradigm and pipeline stage, we separate workflow efficiency, hypothesis generation, candidate prioritization, and validated drug-discovery outcomes. Current evidence shows that LLMs can improve literaturescale synthesis, interface design, molecular ideation, patient–trial matching, pharmacovigilance triage, and structured evidence organization. However, many claims remain based on benchmark evaluation, retrospective analysis, prototype workflows, or case demonstrations rather than prospective experimental or clinical validation. Major limitations include hallucination, data leakage, benchmark saturation, training–test similarity, weak out-of-distribution performance, insufficient wet-lab confirmation, limited route-feasibility analysis, uncertain patentability, model drift, privacy risks, and incomplete auditability. This review therefore proposes an evidencereadiness framework that classifies applications from proof-of-concept to benchmark validation, retrospective external validation, prospective workflow testing, controlled deployment, and regulatory-grade use. By linking application scope, model paradigm, evidence maturity, and risk-aware validation requirements, the article clarifies where LLMs are practically useful today, where claims remain preliminary, and what technical, experimental, clinical, and governance advances are required before LLM outputs can support high-consequence pharmaceutical decision-making.","doi":"10.26434/chemrxiv.15005003/v1","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000238","title":"Virtual cell construction for artificial intelligence‐driven drug discovery","abstract":"Cells are the fundamental units through which genetic variation and pharmacological perturbations influence disease processes and therapeutic responses. However, cellular responses to intervention are strongly shaped by biological context, creating a central challenge for drug discovery: predicting how specific perturbations reshape cellular systems across diverse environments. Recent advances in single-cell and spatial multi-omics technologies, large-scale perturbation profiling and artificial intelligence have made such questions increasingly tractable. These developments have driven the emergence of virtual cells as integrative computational frameworks that represent cellular states, biological context and perturbation responses within unified models. In this review, we discuss the conceptual foundations and modelling paradigms underlying virtual cell systems, including cellular representation learning, multimodal integration, perturbation prediction and mechanistic inference. We further examine how these frameworks support key drug discovery tasks, including target prioritization, drug response prediction and combination therapy design, and we outline the major challenges and future directions for virtual cells as predictive systems for therapeutic discovery.","doi":"10.1111/bph.70585","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000239","title":"ChatMDV: Reducing Technical Barriers in Bioinformatics Analysis using Large Language Models","abstract":"BACKGROUND\nThe rapid advancement in single-cell, spatial omics, imaging, and genomic technologies requires robust analytical and visualisation platforms capable of managing complex biological data. Tools such as Multi-Dimensional Viewer (MDV) offer comprehensive interfaces for data exploration, but often require advanced computational expertise and manual configuration to generate visualisation outputs, limiting accessibility for many users.\n\n\nRESULTS\nWe present ChatMDV, a natural language interface integrated with MDV that enables users to generate high-quality, interactive visualisations and analyses through natural language commands. ChatMDV employs a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline in combination with large language models (LLMs) to translate user queries into executable, reproducible Python code and interactive output. This conversational layer facilitates both exploratory and targeted analyses in diverse biological domains. We demonstrate ChatMDV's capabilities using three datasets of increasing complexity: the Peripheral Blood Mononuclear Cells 3K (PBMC3K) single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) dataset, the lung cancer atlas scRNA-seq dataset included in the Human Cell Atlas and the longitudinal TAURUS study scRNA-seq dataset. Across all use cases, ChatMDV produced high-quality, reproducible visualisations from simple natural language queries, achieving a high semantic success rate between 79% and 97% when visualising the datasets.\n\n\nCONCLUSIONS\nBy bridging the gap between natural language processing and bioinformatics visualisation, ChatMDV reduces technical barriers, enhances reproducibility, and supports more inclusive scientific inquiry. Its modular design and adherence to Findability, Accessibility, Interoperability, and Reuse (FAIR) principles make it a scalable and adaptable framework for accelerating biological data analysis.","doi":"10.1093/gigascience/giag073","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]},{"record_id":"rec_000240","title":"Protein foundation models: a comprehensive survey","abstract":"… , including cell signalling, gene expression regulation, and the … SWING is a novel interaction language model that learns … NeuralPLexer employs a multiscale deep generative model to …","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"]}]

